    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n3507" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Ărĕlas</span></b> ,
		ātis (<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Ărĕlātē</span></b> ,
			ēs, <i><span class="en">Suet. Tib.
			4</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Aus. Clar. Urb. 8</span></i>),
		f., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἀρέλαται</span> or <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἀρέλατον</span>, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a town in Southern Gaul</i>, <i>on the eastern branch of the Rhone</i>, now <i>Arles</i>, <i><span class="en">Caes. B. C. 1, 36</span></i>; cf. Mann. Gall.
			96.—Hence, <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Ărĕlātensis</span></b> ,
			e, adj., <i>Arelatian</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ager,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					10, 42, 57, § 116</span></i>
			; plur., <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Ărĕlāten-ses</span></b> ,
			ium, m., <i>the inhabitants
				of Arelas</i>, <i><span class="en">Dig. 32, 2, 34</span></i>.</div>
